ative Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 8, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2026, "rank": 25, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 290, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2025, "rank": 90, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 8, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2025, "rank": 24, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 288, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500--apparel", "title": "Fortune Sector Leaders: Apparel", "year": 2024, "rank": 1, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2024, "rank": 88, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 9, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2024, "rank": 14, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 16, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 308, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "americas-most-innovative-companies", "title": "America's Most Innovative Companies", "year": 2023, "rank": 23, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2023, "rank": 93, "itemDescription": "Despite falling 10 spots on the Fortune 500, the top apparel brand in the U.S. toughed through a year of continued supply-chain disruptions to post a 4.9% increase in revenue and a 5.6% increase in profit.<br />\r\n<ul><br />\r\n \t<li>Because of global supply-chain woes, Nike experienced an inventory glut in 2022 and was forced to sell many of its products at a discount later on that year.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>The apparel company has recently benefited from the popularity of athleisure and casual fashion, which has come into vogue with the rise of work-from-home.</li><br />\r\n \t<li>Growth in China, its third biggest market by revenue, stalled throughout 2022 owing to COVID-19 lockdowns and boycotts of Western brands. However, after the country relaxed its pandemic restrictions, revenue has begun to improve, with Nike seeing a 11% increase from the second to third quarter of its 2023 fiscal year.</li><br />\r\n</ul>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2022, "rank": 12, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 313, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2022, "rank": 83, "itemDescription": "<span style=\"font-weight: 400\">Nike has been celebrating its 50th anniversary over the past year, and doing so in style: Its revenue grew 19% in 2021, while profits more than doubled. The maker of athletic footwear, apparel, and equipment benefited from a spike in e-commerce sales, and from the fact that people are spending more time wearing \u201cathleisure\u201d attire in the remote-work era. Nike has been one of the more aggressive brands in establishing a metaverse presence: In November 2021, Nike launched a showroom and gaming platform on the popular immersive gaming site Roblox, which has since attracted more than 7 million visitors.\u00a0</span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2021, "rank": 13, "itemDescription": "&nbsp;"}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 321, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Nike was on a tear in its fiscal 2020, with revenue up 7% during the first nine months of the period. Then came the coronavirus. Sales at the world\u2019s largest purveyor of athletic footwear and apparel plunged 38% in the fourth quarter of its fiscal year as retail stores closed, supply chains were disrupted, and sporting events were canceled. CEO John Donahoe, who had only been in the top job since January 2020, continued the company\u2019s drive into e-commerce\u2014a move that helped soften the blow of COVID-19. Digital sales increased nearly 50% for the fiscal year to $5.5 billion. &quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">Nike was on a tear in its fiscal 2020, with revenue up 7% during the first nine months of the period. Then came the coronavirus. Sales at the world\u2019s largest purveyor of athletic footwear and apparel plunged 38% in the fourth quarter of its fiscal year as retail stores closed, supply chains were disrupted, and sporting events were canceled. CEO John Donahoe, who had only been in the top job since January 2020, continued the company\u2019s drive into e-commerce\u2014a move that helped soften the blow of COVID-19. Digital sales increased nearly 50% for the fiscal year to $5.5 billion.</span>"}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2021, "rank": 85, "itemDescription": "<span data-sheets-value=\"{&quot;1&quot;:2,&quot;2&quot;:&quot;Nike was on a tear in its fiscal 2020, with revenue up 7% during the first nine months of the period. Then came the coronavirus. Sales at the world\u2019s largest purveyor of athletic footwear and apparel plunged 38% in the fourth quarter of its fiscal year as retail stores closed, supply chains were disrupted, and sporting events were canceled. CEO John Donahoe, who had only been in the top job since January 2020, continued the company\u2019s drive into e-commerce\u2014a move that helped soften the blow of COVID-19. Digital sales increased nearly 50% for the fiscal year to $5.5 billion. &quot;}\" data-sheets-userformat=\"{&quot;2&quot;:12673,&quot;3&quot;:{&quot;1&quot;:0},&quot;10&quot;:2,&quot;11&quot;:4,&quot;15&quot;:&quot;Calibri&quot;,&quot;16&quot;:11}\">Nike was on a tear in its fiscal 2020, with revenue up 7% during the first nine months of the period. Then came the coronavirus. Sales at the world\u2019s largest purveyor of athletic footwear and apparel plunged 38% in the fourth quarter of its fiscal year as retail stores closed, supply chains were disrupted, and sporting events were canceled. CEO John Donahoe, who had only been in the top job since January 2020, continued the company\u2019s drive into e-commerce\u2014a move that helped soften the blow of COVID-19. Digital sales increased nearly 50% for the fiscal year to $5.5 billion. </span>"}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2020, "rank": 13, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 322, "itemDescription": "It\u2019s hard to imagine Nike, the world\u2019s largest athletic apparel maker, without Mark Parker, the quietly charismatic, running-obsessed athlete-turned-footwear-designer-turned CEO. Parker, who had been chief executive since 2006, announced his departure in the fall of 2019, and was replaced by Nike board member and former eBay CEO John Donahoe in January 2020. (Parker, for now, is staying on as executive chairman.) That said, the hand-off was a smooth one, thanks to a breakout star: China. Nike reported revenue of $1.68 billion in Greater China in September 2019, marking five quarters of double-digit revenue growth in the region, driven in part by its investment in digital commerce. That digital strength has helped fuel the company through the global impact of the COVID-19 pandemic; while revenue was down 38% in the quarter that ended May 31, the company's digital sales were up an impressive 75%."}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2020, "rank": 85, "itemDescription": "It\u2019s hard to imagine Nike, the world\u2019s largest athletic apparel maker, without Mark Parker, the quietly charismatic, running-obsessed athlete-turned-footwear-designer-turned CEO. Parker, who had been chief executive since 2006, announced his departure in the fall of 2019, and was replaced by Nike board member and former eBay CEO John Donahoe in January 2020. (Parker, for now, is staying on as executive chairman.) That said, the hand-off was a smooth one, thanks to a breakout star: China. Nike reported revenue of $1.68 billion in Greater China in September 2019, marking five quarters of double-digit revenue growth in the region, driven in part by its investment in digital commerce. Nike's digital reach helped its China segment continue to perform despite coronavirus-fueled shutdowns in the first few months of 2020."}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2019, "rank": 13, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 341, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2019, "rank": 90, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2018, "rank": 16, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 340, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2018, "rank": 89, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "worlds-most-admired-companies", "title": "World's Most Admired Companies", "year": 2017, "rank": 12, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "global500", "title": "Fortune Global 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 331, "itemDescription": ""}, {"slug": "fortune500", "title": "Fortune 500", "year": 2017, "rank": 88, "itemDescripti